Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:10.070 align:start position:0%
 
and now to the cia  cia offices are trained to lie and to  keep secrets  all in the national interest well a new  book the spymasters  attempts to get at at the heart of the  truth of the cia  and unearth some of those secrets it's  written by chris whipple  cia directors shape history and the  future and chris thank you so much for  joining us  let me start at the very end where you  talk about gina haspel the current  cia director with a quote from someone  saying she'd be a very good  prison camp commandant what did you mean  by that well  you know gina haspel is an absolutely  fascinating character to me  character to me a mystery woman who  flies under the radar  she came out of kentucky a johnny cash  fan and she  cut her teeth as a covert operative in  the back alleys of  africa she then formed a  a relationship her mentor was the  unlikeliest guy you could imagine  jose rodriguez who was the uh  the architect of the whole enhanced  interrogation program  infamously at cia but he became a kind  of feminist mentor to her encouraged her  to go for the  london station as uh at cia and the rest  is history she became  cia director and her record  in my view is really mixed there were  high hopes that she could be the rare  person who could tell donald trump what  he didn't want to hear  but i think she's i think she's really  failed  as the honest broker of information  both to congress and to the american  people  and i can give you many examples of that  but it's very difficult  as we all know for anyone to tell donald  trump hard truths  a recurring theme throughout this book  chris is the importance of the cia  director's relationship with the  president  that's really important how do they all  manage it and how has she managed it do  you think  you know it's uh first of all it's  almost impossible to overstate the  importance of the position of cia  director the cia director is the person  we depend on  to prevent another 911 or lethal  pandemic  and it's almost as you say brian an  impossible  balancing act because you have to tell  the president hard  truths and at the same time you have to  have his ear  the cia director commands an army of  analysts and covert operatives and  paramilitary warriors and lethal drones  but if you don't have a seat at the  table with the president  the whole enterprise is for naught and  this has been true  richard helms for example famously  tried to persuade lbj that the bombing  of north vietnam was  ineffective lbj would have none of it  now fast forward to gina haspel who has  her own difficulties  telling donald trump what he doesn't  want to hear  a question for you chris from rhonda  schwartz rhonda  what is it chris with the cia's  obsession with fidel castro  they've tried to assassinate him they've  tried to invade cuba  what what's up there good question  it had a lot to do with bobby kennedy  one of the  great things about the book was the time  i got to spend  with cynthia helms the widow of the  quintessential cia director richard  helms and cynthia told me and these are  the moments in the book that i love that  where i  really try to humanize the directors  helms would come home  at night and tell her that he had lash  marks on his back  not literally but figuratively because  bobby kennedy had hectored him  all day long about getting rid of fidel  castro  by fair means or foul the kennedys were  obsessed  with that and the other thing that she  told me that i found  just kind of poignant was you know chris  she said they were all asked to do  things they shouldn't have done  and god knows helms was a flawed  character  he did cross the line and he committed  illegal acts for lbj  mh chaos mk ultra all kinds of things  and of course at the end of the day when  the crunch came he stood up to richard  nixon and refused to participate  in the watergate cover-up  and chris you make a good point i think  throughout the book that whatever the ca  has done  and their failures all along their  career their history of the cia  is usually done at the behest of the  president right  that's right and one former director uh  told me brian that there will always be  a cia  you could never abolish it because  presidents would then have no one to  blame  there's a famous lament at langley and  it is in this town there are only  policy successes and intelligence  failures  and of course the cia was blamed for  uh 9 11 but i have chapter and verse in  my chapter  on uh the walk up to 9 11 in which  george tenet and his lieutenants  went over to the white house pounded the  table and told condi rice we had to go  on a war footing now  this was in july of 2001 and yet they  were blamed which is not to say that the  cia hasn't had plenty of screw-ups  and debacles over the years but usually  it's because the president asked them to  do something  all right well chris sipple thank you  very much this is a great book i really  enjoyed it it's a terrific read  once again terrific work for a former  colleague chris whipple thank you so  much  brian great to see you

